  "final_text": "I've rechecked the report against the raw CSVs. My recommendation is not to move more Q3 marketing behind CRAFT yet. Rework the line first. Every figure in the report traced back to the data. The problem is that the VP's two arguments, growth and improving quality, only hold up under the way the report counted them. Count them another way and both go away.\n\n## The VP's two claims, counted both ways\n\n| Claim | As reported | Counted the other way | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| \"Units up 22.4% on August\" | 7,488 → 9,162 units, monthly total | **Per week: 1,872 → 1,832 units, which is down 2.1%** | demand_actuals.csv, `units_actual`, line CRAFT. August has 4 weekly buckets and September has 5. The report's §6 already warns about this. |\n| \"Defects fell 3.21 → 2.51 per 1,000\" | Number of defect reports (NCRs) per 1,000 units: 24 → 23 reports | **Units affected per unit sold: 31.0% → 30.9%, flat.** Units affected went *up* from 2,324 to 2,832. | ncr_records.csv, `qty_affected`, line CRAFT, by `ncr_date` month, divided by units shipped |\n\n**About the two rates:**\n- **Per 1,000 units:** this counts *records*, not units. The rate \"falls\" because one fewer report was spread over a month with an extra week of sales. The size of each report doesn't count at all.\n- **Units affected:** this counts *units*. It's a proxy, because the numerator is production (NCR) units and the denominator is units sold. NCRs are not customer returns (see §6).\n\n## What's underneath\n- **CRAFT's quality is getting worse, not better:**\n  - Scrapped CRAFT units rose from 924 to 1,539 (+67%).\n  - Units affected by weld cracks rose from 228 to 754.\n  - About 31% of CRAFT volume shows up in defect reports, against 5–12% for every other line. It's the highest both months.\n- **\"No other line improved\" depends on the count too.** It's true per report. By units affected, NEST improved from 11.8% to 10.5%.\n- **€4.12M is revenue at list price, not margin.** There's no cost data (§6), and scrapping a product that sells at around €450 is expensive.\n\n**I checked one figure end to end:** CRAFT revenue of €4,124,238 comes from demand_actuals.csv for line CRAFT, weeks 2023-09-02 to 09-30: 95 rows, 9,162 units, multiplied by `base_price_eur` from product_lines.csv. It matches the report exactly. The NCR figures (23 / 2,832, and 24 in August) also match.\n\n## Recommendation\n- **Decision:** rework CRAFT first, and hold off on extra Q3 marketing.\n- **Why:** if you count units rather than reports, about 31% of CRAFT volume shows up in defect reports and the level is flat. Scrap and weld cracks are rising. More demand means more of that scrap.\n- **Risk if we wait:** we lose some premium sales for a while, but demand is flat per week, so there's no surge to miss.\n- **Risk if we push now:** scrap goes up, and defects could start showing up as customer returns.\n- **The other side:** counted per report, defects did fall from 3.21 to 2.51 per 1,000. That's the VP's case, but it comes from the extra week, not better production.\n- **What would change my answer:** this needs to be set in units, not report counts, because merging or splitting reports would move a report-based target without anything actually improving. My suggested bar is:\n  - CRAFT units affected below about 10% of units sold (roughly NEST's level, around 185 units/week at current volume) for 4 weeks in a row\n  - scrapped units falling\n  - weld-crack units back near August's 228/month\n\n  That target is my suggestion, not an agreed threshold, so please confirm it or set your own.\n\nThe marketing budget (€2.03M) can't be broken down by product line (§4), so I can't say what extra spend would actually buy. There were no team definitions for these figures to check against, so I set out each one above.",
